Original commit body from 9dceacba5fd5bebaea27365528d3045f87f1f8c7:

Go's semantic import versioning puts the major version as a trailing path element
from v2 onward. `_process_import_spec` derived a package's identifier as the LAST
element, so `github.com/labstack/echo/v4` bound `v4` and `echo` was never
registered. Every call on such a package then missed `import_aliases`,
`_go_external_import_path` returned None, and the dst fell to the `external`
placeholder -- losing the module hint on an ordinary `calls` edge, so the blast
radius is the Go call graph and everything downstream of it, not one catalogue.
Since Go modules, /vN is the standard for any library at v2 or above.

Derivation moves to `_go_package_identifier`, which also handles gopkg.in's
dotted spelling (`gopkg.in/yaml.v2` -> `yaml`). Both are "a major-version marker
sitting where the package identifier is expected", so both live in one helper --
but they are different rules and are tested separately, because gopkg.in
legitimately uses `.v1` while a Go-module `/v1` element is far more likely to be a
real directory.

OVER-STRIPPING WOULD INVENT A DEFECT WHILE CLOSING ONE, so the guard is
deliberately adversarial: `v2` is a legal package name and `v2beta1` / `v1alpha1`
are routine Kubernetes API package names. Both patterns are anchored and
digits-only, the path-element rule refuses to strip with no earlier element to
fall back to, and the dotted rule is scoped to the gopkg.in host.

