LIKELY ROOT CAUSE OF WI-tivij, open since July with "alias imports?" as an
untested guess. Caddy is `github.com/caddyserver/caddy/v2`;
`caddy.RegisterModule(...)` previously emitted `go:external:0-0:RegisterModule`
and now carries its import path, which is the precondition for the
init -> RegisterModule -> Module.CaddyModule chain that item reports as broken.
NOT CLAIMED TO CLOSE IT: that item reports 16 of 136 markers unreachable, and if
every registration went through the root package all 136 would fail.

THE INSTRUMENT HAD TWO DEFECTS OF ITS OWN, both caught by its own controls and
both fixed before any number above was believed:
  - it keyed each call site on (src, line, dst NAME slot), a discriminator
    derived from the thing under test -- resolving a dst in-repo can re-spell its
    name slot receiver-qualified -- and duly reported a non-destruction failure
    on caddy that was its own;
  - it then paired the two arms' dsts POSITIONALLY within a site, silently
    matching unrelated calls on one line whenever sort order shifted, and
    manufactured 5 regressions that do not exist (`BlobStatter.Stat ->
    external:Digest` is two different calls).
Both are now a multiset difference over a caller-side-only key, which needs no
pairing at all. The real regression count is 0.

NO CROSS-LANGUAGE PARITY TEST, AND HERE IS WHY RATHER THAN SILENCE. The project
rule is that unavoidable per-language logic ships a parity test enumerating every
language. This logic is not merely per-language, it is per-language SEMANTICS:
/vN is a Go module convention and gopkg.in is a Go hosting convention, so a test
asserting either behaviour for python or rust would assert something false. What
DOES generalise is the shape -- "an identifier derived from a path segment can be
defeated by a version marker occupying that segment" -- and that is a question
for the other analyzers, not an assertion about them. It is recorded on INV-javid
as an open question and no other analyzer was read.

